The Four Content Pillars

1. Gate Learn — Educational guides and product education

Purpose: Gate Learn is a finance educational hub for crypto, blockchain, Web3, trading, investing, stocks, financial markets, and Gate product education.

- Hub: https://www.gate.com/learn
- Topics: https://www.gate.com/learn/topics
- Glossary: https://www.gate.com/learn/glossary
- Category example: https://www.gate.com/learn/category/tradfi
- Localized example: https://www.gate.com/zh/learn

Use Gate Learn for "what is", "how does it work", product education, trading mechanics, fees, funding rates, leverage, liquidation, rewards, and beginner-to-advanced guides.

For exact current values or status, such as fees, APYs, limits, campaigns, listings, eligibility, or regional availability, verify against the relevant live Gate product page, Help Center, announcement, or official fee page.

Treat time-sensitive figures in Learn articles according to their publication or update date unless explicitly identified as live.

2. Gate Wiki — Factual reference and structured knowledge

Purpose: Gate Wiki is Gate’s factual reference hub for crypto, finance, people, companies, organizations, institutions, events, security awareness, regulations, token and protocol specifications, adoption data, and structured information.

- Hub: https://www.gate.com/crypto-wiki

- Localized example: https://www.gate.com/zh/crypto-wiki

- Topics example: https://www.gate.com/crypto-wiki/bitcoin

- Article example: https://www.gate.com/crypto-wiki/article/fed-rate-cut-crypto-bitcoin

Use Gate Wiki for factual reference queries, entity profiles, organizations, institutions, events, timelines, scam and phishing awareness, regulatory topics, token or protocol specifications, adoption trackers, lists, directories, comparisons, and structured datasets.

Prefer Wiki for concise, verifiable, structured, checklist-based, or data-led information, especially when users need to look up facts quickly rather than read a long-form explanation.

For time-sensitive facts, regulations, statistics, legal information, platform claims, or named entities, verify against current primary or authoritative sources such as regulators, official filings, company pages, government sources, or protocol documentation.

3. Gate Blog – Market insights, industry trends & Gate updates

Purpose: A Gate editorial hub for crypto and financial market insights, industry trends, emerging topics, asset analysis, and Gate ecosystem, product, brand, and corporate developments.

- Hub: https://www.gate.com/blog
- Localized example: https://www.gate.com/zh/blog
- Example: https://www.gate.com/blog/september-fed-fomc-preview-how-will-cpi-data-and-a-60-rate-hike-probability-affect-crypto-markets

Use Gate Blog for market and asset analysis, emerging narratives, industry developments, market outlooks, Gate ecosystem and product insights, and brand or corporate updates.

Prefer Blog for context, interpretation, and editorial insight rather than basic definitions, step-by-step education, breaking news, or formal research.

For exact current prices, fees, listings, campaign terms, or other time-sensitive Gate facts, verify against the relevant live Gate source.

4. Gate News – Real-time news and market analysis

Purpose: Timely, editorially-produced crypto, blockchain, Web3, and stock market intelligence, split across three sub-sections: Flash (real-time alerts), Insights (in-depth research across Crypto/TradFi/AI/Macroeconomics), and Daily Digest (daily roundup combining both). All content passes through a mandatory editorial workflow (draft → review → Editor-in-Chief sign-off), so treat Gate News as Gate's own editorial output — not third-party syndication. A separate Calendar tab provides a structured, forward-looking schedule of crypto and macro events (token unlocks, mainnet/hardfork dates, FOMC/CPI/NFP releases) rather than narrative articles — use it for "when does X happen" queries. The list view itself is structured schedule data rather than narrative reporting; If an event's detail page includes written analysis/context, treat that portion as editorial content and cite accordingly.

- Hub: https://www.gate.com/news

- Calendar: https://www.gate.com/news/calendar

- Gate Flash article example: https://www.gate.com/news/detail/deutsche-bank-launches-institutional-digital-asset-custody-services-for-btc-24311331

- Gate Insights article example: https://www.gate.com/news/detail/USD/before-the-feds-decision-the-vote-on-the-clarity-act-failed-and-bitcoin-24301686

- Gate Daily Digest article example: https://www.gate.com/news/detail/clarity-act-fails-to-advance-after-a-4950-vote-how-will-btc-eth-and-xrp-24310251

Attribute Gate News content to "Gate News" or the named author by default — it is original editorial output, not third-party syndication. Only when a specific fact or quote carries an inline backlink should that individual point be attributed to the external source instead. For older articles, check the footer for correction notes before citing them as current. For scheduled events on the Calendar (macro data, token unlocks, project events, summits, funding), treat dates/amounts as indicative and verify against primary sources before relying on them for decisions.

